{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2162-04-23 18:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I'm Dr. Lee. You're in the ICU because your blood sugar was extremely high and you're in diabetic ketoacidosis. Do you know what that means?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2162-04-23 18:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yeah, I’ve been here before. It’s when my body starts breaking down fat because it doesn’t have enough insulin, right? I feel like I did last time."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2162-04-23 18:03:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. Your blood sugar was over 1100, your pH was very low, and you had high ketones. We’ve started you on an insulin drip and fluids. Do you know why this might have happened this time?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2162-04-23 18:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "My glucose meter broke, so I couldn’t check my sugars. I was still taking my long-acting insulin, but I only took 4 units of Humalog with meals instead of adjusting it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2162-04-23 18:06:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That makes sense. Not adjusting your fast-acting insulin during illness can lead to DKA. You also had nausea and vomiting—did you think it was a stomach bug?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2162-04-23 18:08:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yeah, I felt sick for a few days. Stomach pain, throwing up. I thought it would pass, but it just got worse."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2162-04-23 18:09:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We checked your labs and urine—no signs of infection. So it’s likely a viral illness combined with not taking enough insulin. We’re giving you IV fluids and potassium, and we’ll keep the insulin drip going until your levels stabilize."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2162-04-23 18:11:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. My chest felt tight earlier—was that from the DKA?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2162-04-23 18:12:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Possibly. Your EKG showed some ST depressions, and your troponin is slightly elevated. But since you don’t have blockages, we think it’s ‘demand ischemia’—your heart was under stress from the high sugars and dehydration."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2162-04-23 18:14:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s not a heart attack?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2162-04-23 18:15:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not a typical one. It’s more like your heart was working too hard. As your DKA improves, the strain should go down. We’ll keep monitoring your EKGs."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2162-04-23 18:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that’s a relief. My kidneys—are they okay? Last time I had issues."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2162-04-23 18:18:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your creatinine was high at 2.4, but that’s likely from dehydration. We’ve stopped your lisinopril for now, and your levels are improving. We’ll restart it once you’re stable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2162-04-23 18:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I haven’t been taking my blood pressure pills regularly either."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2162-04-23 18:21:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll help you get back on track. We’re also watching your potassium—it was very high earlier, so we gave you calcium gluconate to protect your heart."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2162-04-23 18:23:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I remember that shot. It made my face feel warm."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2162-04-23 18:24:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s normal. Your levels are coming down. We’ll keep adjusting your insulin and fluids overnight. Try to rest."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2162-04-24 07:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your blood sugar is coming down, and your acid levels are improving. The insulin drip is helping. How are you feeling?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2162-04-24 07:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Better. Nausea’s gone. Can I have some ice chips?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2162-04-24 07:03:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, you can have ice chips. We’re still holding food until your levels stabilize more. We’re also watching your white blood cell count—it was high but seems to be from the DKA, not infection."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2162-04-24 07:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I haven’t been drinking—just trying to stay in my outpatient program."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2162-04-24 07:06:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good to hear. We’re giving you thiamine and vitamins just in case. Social work will help you reconnect with your program after discharge."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2162-04-24 12:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your blood gas shows your pH is improving—up to 7.33. That’s a big step. We’re going to try switching you to subcutaneous insulin soon."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2162-04-24 12:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So no more IV insulin?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2162-04-24 12:03:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll try, but sometimes the acid levels can rebound. If that happens, we might need to go back on the drip for a bit."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2162-04-24 12:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. I’ll make sure to check my sugars more often when I get home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2162-04-24 16:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We switched you to subcutaneous insulin, but your anion gap reopened. So we’ve restarted the insulin drip for now. It’s not uncommon—your body just needs more time to recover."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2162-04-24 16:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I was doing so well. Is something wrong?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2162-04-24 16:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "No, this is part of the process. Your metabolism is still adjusting. We’ll keep the drip a little longer and try again tomorrow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2162-04-25 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Great news—your bicarb is up to 18 and your gap is closed. We’re switching you back to subcutaneous insulin. You’re also having some hypoglycemia, so we’re giving you IV dextrose until you can eat."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2162-04-25 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I feel shaky sometimes. Is that from low sugar?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2162-04-25 08:03:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, that’s a sign. We’re monitoring closely. Once you can keep food down, we’ll stop the IV sugar."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2162-04-25 14:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your troponin peaked at 0.28 but is now coming down. EKGs are improving too. This supports that it was demand ischemia, not a blockage."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2162-04-25 14:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So I don’t need a stent or anything?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2162-04-25 14:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not right now. We’ll continue aspirin and high-dose statin. You should follow up with cardiology for a stress test later."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2162-04-26 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your creatinine is now 0.7—better than your baseline. We’ve restarted lisinopril. Your blood pressure is a bit high, so we’re adding amlodipine."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2162-04-26 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I guess I need to take my pills more regularly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2162-04-26 09:03:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, and we’re giving you a new glucose meter and strips so you can check your sugars. A diabetes educator will go over how to adjust your insulin."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2162-04-26 15:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your white count dropped to 2.9—mild leukopenia. It’s likely from the viral illness and your history. We’ll check it as an outpatient."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2162-04-26 15:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is that dangerous?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2162-04-26 15:03:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not at this point. It’s expected after DKA. Just avoid sick people and wash your hands."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2162-04-27 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re doing really well. Off IV fluids, eating normally, and your labs are stable. We’re planning for discharge tomorrow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2162-04-27 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s great. What about my insulin? It’s different now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2162-04-27 10:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll be on 15 units of glargine twice daily—down from 18—and continue the sliding scale. The educator will review it with you."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2162-04-27 10:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my blood pressure meds?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2162-04-27 10:07:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Lisinopril and amlodipine. We’ll adjust doses based on your readings. Check them daily."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2162-04-28 11:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "All set for discharge today. You’re on aspirin now too, for heart protection. Any questions?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2162-04-28 11:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Do I need to see a dentist? My teeth hurt."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2162-04-28 11:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we noted poor dentition. Please schedule a dental check-up. Other than that, follow up with your primary doctor, endocrinology, and cardiology."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2162-04-28 11:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Thank you for helping me through this again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 51,
      "time": "2162-04-28 11:07:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Stay on your insulin, check your sugars, and call if you’re sick or can’t keep food down. You’re going home with services to help with follow-up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 52,
      "time": "2162-04-28 14:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Just a final check before you leave. Your vitals are stable, labs are good. You’re cleared to go home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 53,
      "time": "2162-04-28 14:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I feel so much better. I won’t let this happen again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 54,
      "time": "2162-04-28 14:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re here to help. Keep in touch with your care team. Take care."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 55,
      "time": "2162-04-29 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I’m just signing your discharge papers. You officially discharged as of now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 56,
      "time": "2162-04-29 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. I’ll head home now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 57,
      "time": "2162-04-29 08:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Safe travels. Remember to follow up in a week."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 58,
      "time": "2162-04-29 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Quick call to confirm—yes, your discharge is complete as of 8:05 AM."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 59,
      "time": "2162-04-29 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you again, Doctor."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 60,
      "time": "2162-04-29 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Take care of yourself. You’ve made great progress."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2162-04-23 17:48:00",
    "admission_end": "2162-04-29 17:11:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A woman with a history of type 1 diabetes, poorly controlled with prior DKA admissions, alcohol use disorder, hypertension, and depression presented with abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting for several days. She had not been adjusting her sliding scale insulin due to a broken glucose meter and was only taking long-acting insulin. She was found in severe diabetic ketoacidosis with a glucose of 1162, pH of 7.06, and anion gap of 54. She was treated in the ICU with IV fluids, an insulin drip titrated up to 15 units/hour, and electrolyte management. Her course was complicated by transient hypotension requiring brief pressor support, acute kidney injury likely pre-renal from dehydration, and demand ischemia evidenced by ST depressions and a troponin peak of 0.28. She had a transient need for IV dextrose due to hypoglycemia during transition to subcutaneous insulin. Her kidney function improved with creatinine decreasing to 0.6, and she was restarted on lisinopril with addition of amlodipine for blood pressure control. She was discharged home with services on a reduced glargine dose, sliding scale insulin, aspirin, and close follow-up arranged with endocrinology, cardiology, and her outpatient substance use program.",
    "problems": [
      "Type 1 diabetes mellitus with ketoacidosis without coma",
      "Acute kidney failure, unspecified",
      "Other forms of acute ischemic heart disease",
      "Hypotension, unspecified",
      "Type 1 diabetes mellitus with diabetic neuropathy, unspecified",
      "Hyperkalemia",
      "Type 1 diabetes mellitus with unspecified diabetic retinopathy without macular edema",
      "Long term (current) use of insulin",
      "Essential (primary) hypertension",
      "Dehydration",
      "Major depressive disorder, single episode, unspecified",
      "Patient's other noncompliance with medication regimen",
      "Alcohol abuse, uncomplicated",
      "Anemia in other chronic diseases classified elsewhere",
      "Noninfective gastroenteritis and colitis, unspecified"
    ]
  }
}
